  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "IB SL statistics covers descriptive statistics, probability laws, distributions, and correlation — with extensive use of the GDC for computations and hypothesis tests (AI SL).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Descriptive statistics",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "\\[\\bar{x}=\\frac{\\sum f_ix_i}{\\sum f_i}\\quad(\\text{mean from frequency table}),\\quad IQR=Q_3-Q_1.\\]<p>A value is an outlier if \\(x&lt;Q_1-1.5\\,IQR\\) or \\(x&gt;Q_3+1.5\\,IQR.\\)</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Counting principles: permutations &amp; combinations",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>A <strong>permutation</strong> counts arrangements where order matters; a <strong>combination</strong> counts selections where it doesn't. Choosing \\(r\\) objects from \\(n\\) distinct objects: \\[ {}^nP_r=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\quad(\\text{ordered}),\\qquad{}^nC_r=\\binom{n}{r}=\\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\\quad(\\text{unordered}). \\] \\({}^nC_r\\) is exactly the binomial coefficient from Chapter 1's binomial theorem — the number of ways to choose which \\(r\\) of the \\(n\\) factors contribute the \\(b\\) in each term of \\((a+b)^n\\) is what makes \\(\\binom{n}{r}\\) show up there, and the same counting question — how many size-\\(r\\) subsets, or how many size-\\(k\\) \"successes\" among \\(n\\) trials — is exactly what makes \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\) appear again below in the binomial distribution.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "workedExampleCard",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked Example 4.A — Permutation vs combination",
          "ar": null
        },
        "problem": {
          "en": "(a) In how many ways can a president, secretary, and treasurer be chosen from 8 club members? (b) In how many ways can a 3-person committee (no distinct roles) be chosen from the same 8 members?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p>(a) Order matters (different roles), so this is a permutation: \\[ {}^8P_3=\\frac{8!}{5!}=8\\times7\\times6=336. \\]</p><p>(b) Order doesn't matter (just a set of 3 people), so this is a combination: \\[ {}^8C_3=\\frac{8!}{3!\\,5!}=\\frac{336}{6}=56. \\] Exactly \\(3!=6\\) times smaller than (a), since each unordered committee of 3 corresponds to \\(3!=6\\) different role-assignments.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Probability",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "\\[P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B),\\quad P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}.\\]<p>Independent: \\(P(A\\cap B)=P(A)\\cdot P(B).\\) Mutually exclusive: \\(P(A\\cap B)=0.\\)</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Distributions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Binomial</strong> \\(X\\sim B(n,p)\\): \\(P(X=k)=\\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k},\\;\\mu=np,\\;\\sigma^2=np(1-p).\\)</p><p><strong>Normal</strong> \\(X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\): \\(z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}.\\) Use GDC: <code>normalcdf</code>, <code>invNorm</code>.</p><p><strong>Bivariate (AI SL):</strong> Pearson's correlation \\(r\\); LSRL \\(\\hat{y}=ax+b\\). Use GDC for linear regression.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · The Binomial distribution",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "ibslBinomCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "A bar chart of the Binomial probability distribution P of X equals k, for adjustable n and p, with a movable highlighted outcome k",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "ibslBinomDataBtn",
          "panelId": "ibslBinomDataPanel",
          "descId": "ibslBinomDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "ibslBinomDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "P(X = k) for every k from 0 to n",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            { "en": "k", "ar": null },
            { "en": "P(X = k)", "ar": null }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "ibslBinomN",
            "label": { "en": "trials <b>n</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": 5, "max": 30, "value": 12, "step": 1,
            "valueDisplayId": "ibslBinomNVal",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "Number of trials n", "ar": null }
          },
          {
            "id": "ibslBinomP",
            "label": { "en": "success prob. <b>p</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": 5, "max": 95, "value": 30, "step": 5,
            "valueDisplayId": "ibslBinomPVal",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "Success probability p, as a percentage", "ar": null }
          },
          {
            "id": "ibslBinomK",
            "label": { "en": "highlight <b>k</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": 0, "max": 30, "value": 4, "step": 1,
            "valueDisplayId": "ibslBinomKVal",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "k, the highlighted outcome for P(X = k)", "ar": null }
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "ibslBinomMean",
            "label": { "en": "mean μ = np", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "indigo",
            "value": "3.60"
          },
          {
            "id": "ibslBinomPK",
            "label": { "en": "P(X = k)", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "0.2311"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "Default n = 12, p = 0.30, k = 4 matches the Practice Problem below exactly (P(X=4) ≈ 0.2311, mean = 3.6). Each bar's height is \\(\\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k}\\) — the counting factor \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\) from the combinations definition above, multiplied by the probability of any one specific sequence with exactly k successes. Drag k past n and the bar (and probability) simply vanish — there's no such outcome.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · The Normal distribution",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "ibslNormCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "A bell-shaped Normal distribution curve with adjustable mean and standard deviation, shaded up to a movable x value",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "ibslNormDataBtn",
          "panelId": "ibslNormDataPanel",
          "descId": "ibslNormDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "ibslNormDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "Key points at μ ± kσ and their cumulative probabilities",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            { "en": "point", "ar": null },
            { "en": "x", "ar": null },
            { "en": "P(X ≤ x)", "ar": null }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "ibslNormMu",
            "label": { "en": "mean <b>μ</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": 0,
            "max": 20,
            "value": 10,
            "step": 1,
            "valueDisplayId": "ibslNormMuVal",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "Mean mu", "ar": null }
          },
          {
            "id": "ibslNormSigma",
            "label": { "en": "std dev <b>σ</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": 1,
            "max": 6,
